  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Mercantour is one of the natural jewels of the Southern Alps — but it's also a national park, with one rule that has no exceptions: dogs are strictly banned from the park's core zone, even on a leash or carried in your arms. This ban, common to every French national park, has been in place since the Mercantour was created in 1979.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Only livestock-protection or herding dogs, and guide dogs for the blind or assistance dogs, are allowed in the core zone. Everyone else should head for the buffer zone (the "aire d'adhésion"), which is less strictly regulated and where dogs on a leash remain welcome on many trails. Before you set off, check carefully whether your chosen route crosses the core zone or stays within the buffer zone — a map of the zones is available on the national park's website.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: head for the valleys and trails in the buffer zone — just as spectacular, and far more numerous than the core zone, which covers only a tiny fraction of French territory.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The resorts and villages around the Mercantour (the Tinée and Vésubie valleys, Ubaye) offer plenty of hike starting points within the buffer zone, accessible with a dog on a leash.</p>
